### (1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format

```gherkin
Feature: Control all smart devices from a single app

  Scenario: Add a new smart device to the app
    Given I have opened the smart home app
    When I navigate to the "Add Device" section
    And I select the type of device I want to add
    And I follow the on-screen instructions to add the device
    Then the new device should appear in my list of devices

  Scenario: Remove a smart device from the app
    Given I have opened the smart home app
    And I have a device already added
    When I navigate to the device settings
    And I select the "Remove Device" option
    Then the device should no longer appear in my list of devices

  Scenario: Control a smart device from the app
    Given I have opened the smart home app
    And I have a device already added
    When I select the device from my list of devices
    And I choose a control option (e.g., turn on/off, adjust settings)
    Then the device should respond to the control command

  Scenario: View the status of a smart device
    Given I have opened the smart home app
    And I have a device already added
    When I select the device from my list of devices
    Then I should see the current status of the device (e.g., on/off, current settings)
```

### (2) Step Definitions in Python

```python
from behave import given, when, then

@given('I have opened the smart home app')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.open()

@when('I navigate to the "Add Device" section')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.go_to_add_device_section()

@when('I select the type of device I want to add')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.select_device_type()

@when('I follow the on-screen instructions to add the device')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.follow_add_device_instructions()

@then('the new device should appear in my list of devices')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.app.is_device_in_list()

@given('I have a device already added')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.add_device_if_not_exists()

@when('I navigate to the device settings')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.go_to_device_settings()

@when('I select the "Remove Device" option')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.remove_device()

@then('the device should no longer appear in my list of devices')
def step_impl(context):
    assert not context.app.is_device_in_list()

@when('I select the device from my list of devices')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.select_device()

@when('I choose a control option (e.g., turn on/off, adjust settings)')
def step_impl(context):
    context.app.control_device()

@then('the device should respond to the control command')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.app.is_device_responding()

@then('I should see the current status of the device (e.g., on/off, current settings)')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.app.get_device_status() is not None
```

### (3) Validation Checklist

- [ ] Verify the app opens successfully.
- [ ] Verify navigation to the "Add Device" section works.
- [ ] Verify the selection of device type is functional.
- [ ] Verify following on-screen instructions adds the device.
- [ ] Verify the new device appears in the list of devices.
- [ ] Verify the app can detect if a device is already added.
- [ ] Verify navigation to device settings works.
- [ ] Verify the "Remove Device" option removes the device.
- [ ] Verify the removed device no longer appears in the list.
- [ ] Verify selecting a device from the list works.
- [ ] Verify control options (e.g., turn on/off, adjust settings) are functional.
- [ ] Verify the device responds to control commands.
- [ ] Verify the current status of the device is displayed correctly.

**(1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format:**

```gherkin
Feature: Generate OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint from a Tabular Data Package

  Scenario: Generate API endpoint from valid Tabular Data Package
    Given I have a valid Tabular Data Package
    When I request to generate an OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint
    Then I should receive a valid API endpoint URL

  Scenario: Generate API endpoint from invalid Tabular Data Package
    Given I have an invalid Tabular Data Package
    When I request to generate an OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint
    Then I should receive an error message indicating the package is invalid

  Scenario: Generate API endpoint from empty Tabular Data Package
    Given I have an empty Tabular Data Package
    When I request to generate an OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint
    Then I should receive an error message indicating the package is empty
```

**(2) Step Definitions in Python:**

```python
from behave import given, when, then
import requests

@given('I have a valid Tabular Data Package')
def step_given_valid_tabular_data_package(context):
    context.tabular_data_package = {
        # Add valid Tabular Data Package details here
    }

@given('I have an invalid Tabular Data Package')
def step_given_invalid_tabular_data_package(context):
    context.tabular_data_package = {
        # Add invalid Tabular Data Package details here
    }

@given('I have an empty Tabular Data Package')
def step_given_empty_tabular_data_package(context):
    context.tabular_data_package = {}

@when('I request to generate an OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint')
def step_when_request_generate_api_endpoint(context):
    response = requests.post('http://example.com/api/generate', json=context.tabular_data_package)
    context.response = response

@then('I should receive a valid API endpoint URL')
def step_then_receive_valid_api_endpoint(context):
    assert context.response.status_code == 200
    assert 'api_endpoint' in context.response.json()

@then('I should receive an error message indicating the package is invalid')
def step_then_receive_invalid_package_error(context):
    assert context.response.status_code == 400
    assert context.response.json().get('error') == 'Invalid Tabular Data Package'

@then('I should receive an error message indicating the package is empty')
def step_then_receive_empty_package_error(context):
    assert context.response.status_code == 400
    assert context.response.json().get('error') == 'Empty Tabular Data Package'
```

**(3) Validation Checklist:**

- [ ] Verify the app accepts a valid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the app rejects an invalid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the app rejects an empty Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the app generates a valid OpenRefine reconciliation API endpoint URL for a valid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the app returns an error message for an invalid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the app returns an error message for an empty Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the response status code is 200 for a valid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the response status code is 400 for an invalid Tabular Data Package.
- [ ] Verify the response status code is 400 for an empty Tabular Data Package.

**1. Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format:**

```gherkin
Feature: DSpace Integration

  Scenario: Successful integration of data-packaged data with DSpace
    Given I have a valid data package
    When I integrate the data package with DSpace
    Then the data should be successfully integrated into the DSpace pipeline

  Scenario: Integration with invalid data package
    Given I have an invalid data package
    When I attempt to integrate the data package with DSpace
    Then I should receive an error message indicating the data package is invalid

  Scenario: Integration with missing data package
    Given I do not have a data package
    When I attempt to integrate with DSpace
    Then I should receive an error message indicating the data package is missing

  Scenario: Integration with DSpace when DSpace is down
    Given I have a valid data package
    And DSpace is down
    When I attempt to integrate the data package with DSpace
    Then I should receive an error message indicating DSpace is unavailable
```

**2. Step Definitions in Python:**

```python
from behave import given, when, then

@given('I have a valid data package')
def step_given_valid_data_package(context):
    context.data_package = "valid_data_package"

@given('I have an invalid data package')
def step_given_invalid_data_package(context):
    context.data_package = "invalid_data_package"

@given('I do not have a data package')
def step_given_no_data_package(context):
    context.data_package = None

@given('DSpace is down')
def step_given_dspace_is_down(context):
    context.dspace_status = "down"

@when('I integrate the data package with DSpace')
def step_when_integrate_with_dspace(context):
    if context.data_package == "valid_data_package" and context.dspace_status != "down":
        context.integration_result = "success"
    elif context.data_package == "invalid_data_package":
        context.integration_result = "error_invalid_package"
    elif context.data_package is None:
        context.integration_result = "error_missing_package"
    elif context.dspace_status == "down":
        context.integration_result = "error_dspace_unavailable"

@then('the data should be successfully integrated into the DSpace pipeline')
def step_then_successful_integration(context):
    assert context.integration_result == "success"

@then('I should receive an error message indicating the data package is invalid')
def step_then_error_invalid_package(context):
    assert context.integration_result == "error_invalid_package"

@then('I should receive an error message indicating the data package is missing')
def step_then_error_missing_package(context):
    assert context.integration_result == "error_missing_package"

@then('I should receive an error message indicating DSpace is unavailable')
def step_then_error_dspace_unavailable(context):
    assert context.integration_result == "error_dspace_unavailable"
```

**3. Validation Checklist:**

- [ ] Verify that a valid data package can be successfully integrated with DSpace.
- [ ] Verify that an invalid data package results in an error message indicating the data package is invalid.
- [ ] Verify that attempting to integrate without a data package results in an error message indicating the data package is missing.
- [ ] Verify that attempting to integrate with DSpace when DSpace is down results in an error message indicating DSpace is unavailable.
- [ ] Ensure that the integration process handles all edge cases and error conditions appropriately.
- [ ] Validate that the system logs appropriate messages for successful and failed integration attempts.
- [ ] Confirm that the integration process does not leave any partial or corrupt data in DSpace in case of failure.

### (1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format

```gherkin
Feature: Link datasets with the project DMP

  Scenario: Successfully link a dataset with the project DMP
    Given I am a logged-in depositor
    And I have a dataset ready to be linked
    And I have access to the project DMP
    When I link the dataset with the project DMP
    Then the dataset should be linked with the project DMP
    And I should see a confirmation message

  Scenario: Attempt to link a dataset without access to the project DMP
    Given I am a logged-in depositor
    And I have a dataset ready to be linked
    And I do not have access to the project DMP
    When I attempt to link the dataset with the project DMP
    Then I should see an error message indicating lack of access

  Scenario: Attempt to link a dataset that is already linked
    Given I am a logged-in depositor
    And I have a dataset that is already linked to the project DMP
    When I attempt to link the dataset with the project DMP again
    Then I should see an error message indicating the dataset is already linked
```

### (2) Step Definitions in Python

```python
from behave import given, when, then

@given('I am a logged-in depositor')
def step_impl(context):
    context.user = login_as_depositor()

@given('I have a dataset ready to be linked')
def step_impl(context):
    context.dataset = get_ready_dataset()

@given('I have access to the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    context.dmp_access = check_dmp_access(context.user)

@given('I do not have access to the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    context.dmp_access = False

@given('I have a dataset that is already linked to the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    context.dataset = get_already_linked_dataset()

@when('I link the dataset with the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    if context.dmp_access:
        context.link_result = link_dataset_to_dmp(context.dataset)
    else:
        context.link_result = 'error_no_access'

@when('I attempt to link the dataset with the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    if context.dmp_access:
        context.link_result = link_dataset_to_dmp(context.dataset)
    else:
        context.link_result = 'error_no_access'

@then('the dataset should be linked with the project DMP')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.link_result == 'success'

@then('I should see a confirmation message')
def step_impl(context):
    assert get_confirmation_message() == 'Dataset successfully linked to DMP'

@then('I should see an error message indicating lack of access')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.link_result == 'error_no_access'
    assert get_error_message() == 'You do not have access to the project DMP'

@then('I should see an error message indicating the dataset is already linked')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.link_result == 'error_already_linked'
    assert get_error_message() == 'This dataset is already linked to the project DMP'
```

### (3) Validation Checklist

- [ ] Verify that the user is logged in as a depositor.
- [ ] Verify that the dataset is ready to be linked.
- [ ] Verify that the user has access to the project DMP.
- [ ] Verify that the dataset can be linked to the project DMP.
- [ ] Verify that a confirmation message is displayed upon successful linking.
- [ ] Verify that an error message is displayed if the user does not have access to the project DMP.
- [ ] Verify that an error message is displayed if the dataset is already linked to the project DMP.

**(1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format:**

```gherkin
Feature: Data Migration from Older Version of NeuroHub to Current Version

  Scenario: Successful data migration
    Given the older version of NeuroHub is running
    And the current version of NeuroHub is running
    When I initiate the data migration process
    Then the data should be successfully migrated to the current version
    And the data integrity should be maintained

  Scenario: Data migration with missing data
    Given the older version of NeuroHub is running
    And the current version of NeuroHub is running
    And some data is missing in the older version
    When I initiate the data migration process
    Then the migration should complete with warnings about missing data
    And the existing data should be successfully migrated to the current version

  Scenario: Data migration with corrupted data
    Given the older version of NeuroHub is running
    And the current version of NeuroHub is running
    And some data is corrupted in the older version
    When I initiate the data migration process
    Then the migration should fail with an error message about corrupted data
    And no data should be migrated to the current version

  Scenario: Data migration with network interruption
    Given the older version of NeuroHub is running
    And the current version of NeuroHub is running
    When I initiate the data migration process
    And the network connection is interrupted
    Then the migration should pause and resume once the connection is restored
    And the data should be successfully migrated to the current version
```

**(2) Step Definitions in Python:**

```python
from behave import given, when, then

@given('the older version of NeuroHub is running')
def step_impl(context):
    context.older_version_running = True

@given('the current version of NeuroHub is running')
def step_impl(context):
    context.current_version_running = True

@when('I initiate the data migration process')
def step_impl(context):
    if context.older_version_running and context.current_version_running:
        context.migration_initiated = True

@then('the data should be successfully migrated to the current version')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.migration_initiated is True
    assert context.data_integrity_maintained is True

@then('the data integrity should be maintained')
def step_impl(context):
    context.data_integrity_maintained = True

@given('some data is missing in the older version')
def step_impl(context):
    context.data_missing = True

@then('the migration should complete with warnings about missing data')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.data_missing is True
    context.migration_completed_with_warnings = True

@then('the existing data should be successfully migrated to the current version')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.migration_initiated is True
    assert context.data_integrity_maintained is True

@given('some data is corrupted in the older version')
def step_impl(context):
    context.data_corrupted = True

@then('the migration should fail with an error message about corrupted data')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.data_corrupted is True
    context.migration_failed = True

@then('no data should be migrated to the current version')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.migration_failed is True

@when('the network connection is interrupted')
def step_impl(context):
    context.network_interrupted = True

@then('the migration should pause and resume once the connection is restored')
def step_impl(context):
    if context.network_interrupted:
        context.migration_paused = True
        context.network_interrupted = False
        context.migration_resumed = True

@then('the data should be successfully migrated to the current version')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.migration_resumed is True
    assert context.data_integrity_maintained is True
```

**(3) Validation Checklist:**

- [ ] Verify the older version of NeuroHub is running.
- [ ] Verify the current version of NeuroHub is running.
- [ ] Initiate the data migration process.
- [ ] Check if the data is successfully migrated to the current version.
- [ ] Ensure data integrity is maintained after migration.
- [ ] Verify behavior when some data is missing in the older version.
- [ ] Ensure migration completes with warnings about missing data.
- [ ] Verify behavior when some data is corrupted in the older version.
- [ ] Ensure migration fails with an error message about corrupted data.
- [ ] Verify no data is migrated when there is corrupted data.
- [ ] Simulate network interruption during migration.
- [ ] Ensure migration pauses and resumes once the connection is restored.
- [ ] Verify data is successfully migrated after network interruption.

### (1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format

```gherkin
Feature: Specify SQL Schema

  Scenario: User specifies a valid SQL schema string
    Given the user has access to the schema input field
    When the user inputs a valid SQL schema string
    And submits the schema
    Then the system should accept the schema
    And display a success message

  Scenario: User specifies an invalid SQL schema string
    Given the user has access to the schema input field
    When the user inputs an invalid SQL schema string
    And submits the schema
    Then the system should reject the schema
    And display an error message

  Scenario: User submits an empty SQL schema string
    Given the user has access to the schema input field
    When the user inputs an empty SQL schema string
    And submits the schema
    Then the system should reject the schema
    And display an error message
```

### (2) Step Definitions in Python

```python
from behave import given, when, then
from hamcrest import assert_that, equal_to

@given('the user has access to the schema input field')
def step_impl(context):
    context.schema_input_field = True

@when('the user inputs a valid SQL schema string')
def step_impl(context):
    context.schema_string = "CREATE TABLE test (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100));"

@when('the user inputs an invalid SQL schema string')
def step_impl(context):
    context.schema_string = "INVALID SQL SCHEMA"

@when('the user inputs an empty SQL schema string')
def step_impl(context):
    context.schema_string = ""

@when('submits the schema')
def step_impl(context):
    if context.schema_string == "":
        context.response = "error"
        context.message = "Schema cannot be empty"
    elif context.schema_string.startswith("CREATE TABLE"):
        context.response = "success"
        context.message = "Schema accepted"
    else:
        context.response = "error"
        context.message = "Invalid schema"

@then('the system should accept the schema')
def step_impl(context):
    assert_that(context.response, equal_to("success"))

@then('display a success message')
def step_impl(context):
    assert_that(context.message, equal_to("Schema accepted"))

@then('the system should reject the schema')
def step_impl(context):
    assert_that(context.response, equal_to("error"))

@then('display an error message')
def step_impl(context):
    assert_that(context.message, equal_to("Invalid schema"))
```

### (3) Validation Checklist

- [ ] Verify the user has access to the schema input field.
- [ ] Validate that a valid SQL schema string is accepted.
- [ ] Validate that an invalid SQL schema string is rejected.
- [ ] Validate that an empty SQL schema string is rejected.
- [ ] Ensure a success message is displayed for a valid schema.
- [ ] Ensure an error message is displayed for an invalid schema.
- [ ] Ensure an error message is displayed for an empty schema.

### (1) Gherkin Scenarios in .feature format

```gherkin
Feature: Upload and tag staff generated working papers

  Scenario: Successfully upload and tag a working paper
    Given I am an assistant archivist
    When I upload a working paper
    And I tag the working paper with relevant tags
    Then the working paper should be available for staff and researchers
    And the working paper should be searchable by the tags

  Scenario: Upload a working paper without tags
    Given I am an assistant archivist
    When I upload a working paper without tags
    Then I should see an error message indicating that tags are required

  Scenario: Upload a working paper with invalid file format
    Given I am an assistant archivist
    When I attempt to upload a working paper with an invalid file format
    Then I should see an error message indicating that the file format is not supported

  Scenario: Search for a working paper by tag
    Given a working paper is uploaded and tagged
    When I search for the working paper using a tag
    Then I should see the working paper in the search results
```

### (2) Step Definitions in Python

```python
from behave import given, when, then

@given('I am an assistant archivist')
def step_impl(context):
    context.user_role = 'assistant archivist'

@when('I upload a working paper')
def step_impl(context):
    context.upload_status = 'success'
    context.tags = ['relevant', 'tags']

@when('I tag the working paper with relevant tags')
def step_impl(context):
    context.tag_status = 'success'

@then('the working paper should be available for staff and researchers')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.upload_status == 'success'
    assert context.tag_status == 'success'

@then('the working paper should be searchable by the tags')
def step_impl(context):
    assert 'relevant' in context.tags
    assert 'tags' in context.tags

@when('I upload a working paper without tags')
def step_impl(context):
    context.upload_status = 'success'
    context.tags = []

@then('I should see an error message indicating that tags are required')
def step_impl(context):
    assert not context.tags
    context.error_message = 'Tags are required'
    assert context.error_message == 'Tags are required'

@when('I attempt to upload a working paper with an invalid file format')
def step_impl(context):
    context.upload_status = 'failed'
    context.error_message = 'Invalid file format'

@then('I should see an error message indicating that the file format is not supported')
def step_impl(context):
    assert context.upload_status == 'failed'
    assert context.error_message == 'Invalid file format'

@given('a working paper is uploaded and tagged')
def step_impl(context):
    context.upload_status = 'success'
    context.tags = ['relevant', 'tags']

@when('I search for the working paper using a tag')
def step_impl(context):
    context.search_results = ['working_paper_1']

@then('I should see the working paper in the search results')
def step_impl(context):
    assert 'working_paper_1' in context.search_results
```

### (3) Validation Checklist

- [ ] Verify that the user is an assistant archivist.
- [ ] Verify that a working paper can be uploaded.
- [ ] Verify that tags can be added to the working paper.
- [ ] Verify that the working paper is available for staff and researchers after upload.
- [ ] Verify that the working paper is searchable by the tags.
- [ ] Verify that an error message is shown when uploading a working paper without tags.
- [ ] Verify that an error message is shown when uploading a working paper with an invalid file format.
- [ ] Verify that a working paper can be found using a tag in the search functionality.

